WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.759
Match the accelerator to the memory and compute profile of the job.

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:14.503
VRAM must hold model weights, context cache, activations, runtime overhead, and, for training or fine-tuning, optimizer and gradient state.

00:00:14.503 --> 00:00:18.982
Model file size alone is not a complete requirement.

00:00:18.982 --> 00:00:27.300
Longer context, larger batches, vision inputs, and concurrent sequences can raise memory use after the model has loaded.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.206
GPU generation affects supported numerical formats, software compatibility, and performance.

00:00:32.206 --> 00:00:38.391
Memory bandwidth influences how quickly large model weights can be moved during inference.

00:00:38.391 --> 00:00:43.296
Raw compute matters more for some training and image workloads.

00:00:43.296 --> 00:00:52.468
Review measurements from a comparable model and runtime rather than assuming that a higher product number is always more economical.

00:00:52.468 --> 00:01:02.065
A single high-memory GPU is often easier to use than several smaller devices when one model must fit as a unit.

00:01:02.065 --> 00:01:12.943
Multi-GPU instances can support model parallelism, larger training jobs, or multiple replicas, but the framework must distribute work effectively and interconnect performance can matter.

00:01:12.943 --> 00:01:17.848
Several GPUs do not automatically create one shared memory pool.

00:01:17.848 --> 00:01:22.754
Inference usually prioritizes memory fit, latency, throughput, and serving efficiency.

00:01:22.754 --> 00:01:27.233
Fine-tuning may require much more memory and checkpoint storage.

00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:34.698
Quantization can make inference practical on a smaller rental, while parameter-efficient tuning can reduce training requirements.

00:01:34.698 --> 00:01:39.177
Vision and multimodal models add image-processing and memory demands.

00:01:39.177 --> 00:01:48.348
Test the smallest plausible configuration with real prompts, context, and batches, then move upward only when measurements identify a constraint.
